A||||9||bandit1||pulling a 2.8 where!?!?!?
Z||000000||bandit1||08-03-99||09:18 PM||||Is it true that the only way to pull a 2.8 is from the bottom??  
Z||000001||Cooter||08-03-99||10:44 PM||||No.
Z||000002||lowCG||08-03-99||11:30 PM||||It's just that it's  easier that way; the whole package drops on a jack,while the car sits up on jackstands.It's not that hard to do,did my clutch on a Sunday afternoon,drove it to work the next day.<br>Once heard about these two guys(from one of them,actually),in some rural area,taking a FWD motor out through the top,they cut all kinds of stuff,didn't even think to drop it out the bottom.Hearing "We pulled that sucker" is what I remember most.
Z||000003||ChadMan||08-04-99||07:35 AM||||Is it possible to do it without a hoist?  I've heard you have to raise the rear end of the car approximately 4 feet.  I feel stupid asking, but I can't figure out how I could do this myself.<p>
Z||000004||Formula||08-04-99||11:28 AM||||if you delete files your laptop will get lighter.  SO....... Delete everything out of your car's cpu and you can lift the car with one hand and pull the motor out with the other
Z||000005||lowCG||08-04-99||11:10 PM||||When I did the clutch,it was in an 1896 carriage house/stable,with no hoist,just me,an old floor jack,and a couple of jackstands,some blocks of wood.<br>I raised the car enough to disconnect everything,supported the engine/tranny/cradle package,and then raised the car the rest of the way.
Z||000006||RICK||08-05-99||07:20 AM||||LOWCG..  I'm going to be pulling v-6 out of my Fiero and I have been wondering the same thing...how is the best way to keep raising the car to the proper height to get the motor out.  Did you jack up the front, block it, jack up the back, block it, jack up the front more, block it (you get the picture)?  If this is the way you did it, how far do you you raise the car before switching to the other end of the car?<br>Thanks for your help<br>
Z||000007||lowCG||08-05-99||08:28 PM||||The front,as I recall,stays put on the floor,might even take off the front wheels,and support the car with wood blocks in front to get it lower,and get the rear higher.I found a point where the car would see-saw,allowing for an easier install/allignment of mounting bolts.<br>I do remember a struggle to get the motor up onto something,then it was easy to swing the car down,positioned correctly with the motor/tranny combo.Once you get one bolt in,it's pretty easy to get the rest.
Z||000008||Eric||08-05-99||08:49 PM||||I used a see-saw method too.  I never would have tried it on my own, but it does work.  The pivot point, after removing some engine internals, was about at the halfway point between the hubs and we just rocked it up onto more and more blocks.
Z||000009||batboy||08-05-99||09:52 PM||||Here is the URL for the V8 Archie method of Fiero engine removal. There seems to be a number of different ways to pull/drop a Fiero engine, depending on personal preference.<p>http://www.v8archie.com/arch8.htm
